chmod-dirs-and-files.judge_reason:
The command is: find public/ -type d -exec chmod 755 {} \; -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;

Primaries in find are joined by an implicit AND. So the expression reads: -type d AND -exec chmod 755 AND -type f AND -exec chmod 644. A path cannot be both a directory and a regular file, so the second -type f always fails after the first -type d succeeds.

For a directory, -type d is true, chmod 755 runs and returns true, then -type f is false, so nothing more happens. Directories therefore get 755.

For a file, -type d is false, so the expression short-circuits and the rest never runs. Files are never chmodded to 644.

So the command only sets directories to 755 and leaves regular files untouched, which is only half of the task.

{"verdict":"incorrect","reason":"The implicit AND between primaries means -type d ... -type f can never both be true, so the chmod 644 never runs. Only directories are changed and files are left untouched."}
